The '''GO AI Hub''' gives GO Consortium members an AI agent ([https://www.anthropic.com/claude-code Claude Code]) wired to the tools a curator uses: ontology lookup, the literature, protein databases, the standard annotation set and the GO-CAM model store (Noctua). You work with it in plain language, in your browser. It is experimental and shared: read what it reports, and know where your work is safe (below).

== Getting an account & logging in ==

* '''Who may sign in.''' Access is a GO permission, not an account you create: your GitHub login must be listed in the GO Consortium's user registry (<code>metadata/users.yaml</code> in [https://github.com/geneontology/go-site go-site]) with the <code>go-ai-hub</code> authorization. To be added, contact Kimberly Van Auken or Pascale Gaudet; the hub picks the change up within the hour.
* '''How to sign in.''' Go to [https://ai.geneontology.org ai.geneontology.org] and choose ''Sign in with GitHub''. You sign in with your GitHub account, not an Anthropic one. A login that is not yet listed is refused with a plain "403 Forbidden" page; that means "not listed", not a broken account.
* '''Your GitHub sign-in is also the agent's.''' The hub passes a GitHub token into your session so that the agent can open pull requests and issues '''under your GitHub name''' without a second login. Treat anything it posts as posted by you, and read it.
* '''First login.''' You pick a starter environment once: ''Researcher'' (recommended: the open-ended biocuration playground) or ''Tutorial'' (guided workshop exercises). Later logins go straight to your environment.
* '''Sessions.''' A sign-in lasts 14 days in your browser. Your files stay in your home directory between sessions.

== Software updates ==

* The agent (Claude Code) updates itself on the hub, hourly, to the newest release that is at least two days old, and only while no session is mid-turn. The models follow: sessions run on the current Opus model, falling back to Sonnet when Opus is unavailable.
* The skills (below) update within minutes of a change being merged in their repository. Nothing to do on your side; a running conversation picks up the new text the next time it uses the skill.
* Operators sometimes restart the hub. That ends every running session, including the agent and any terminal; files on disk are kept. Announcements appear in the "What's new" box at the start of your next session, and the full list is in <code>~/.go-jupyter/news/NEWS.md</code>.

== General instructions ==

=== Your first session ===

# After sign-in you land in JupyterLab. On the launcher page, under ''AI Tools'', click the '''GO Agent''' tile. That opens a terminal with the agent running. (A plain terminal, ''File → New → Terminal'', shows a banner: ENTER starts the agent, T then ENTER gives you a shell.)
# A good first question is: ''What can you help me with here?'' The agent introduces itself and its tools.
# The first time you use the model store, the agent asks you to sign in to '''noctua-dev''' with your '''ORCID''' and paste the token it shows you. The token is personal: models you make are attributed to it, and it expires. The agent saves it in your home for later sessions. There is no shared token.

=== What you can ask for ===

* ''What do we know about Epe1 in fission yeast?'' (existing annotations, UniProt, papers)
* ''Find GO terms for serotonin biosynthesis'' or ''what is the CHEBI term for melatonin?''
* ''Review GO-CAM model gomodel:…'': a summary, or a diagram of the causal chain
* ''Draft a model for this pathway'': activities, inputs, locations and causal relations, following GO-CAM best practice
* ''Find recent papers on the molecular function of Trhn and summarize the evidence''
* ''Save this model to the drop box'' (see below)

=== Where your work is safe ===

Four places, and only one of them is a real save.

{| class="wikitable"
! Where !! What it means !! Safe?
|-
| Your home directory on the hub || your working space (notes, drafts, diagrams) || until the machine is rebuilt; nothing is backed up, so download what you want to keep
|-
| '''noctua-dev''' || a proving ground for building and testing models || '''no''': models there can vanish at a server restart
|-
| '''go-cam-drop-box, pull request open''' || your model, complete, queued for GO Central review || '''yes, this is the save'''
|-
| go-cam-drop-box, merged || accepted; copied into production <code>noctua-models</code> under the same id at the next Noctua maintenance window || yes, and it has landed
|}

* To save: ask the agent to ''save this model to the drop box''. It opens a pull request in [https://github.com/geneontology/go-cam-drop-box go-cam-drop-box] under your GitHub name, carrying the model in two formats under its noctua-dev id. The pull request is validated automatically and reviewed by GO Central.
* A model must '''exist on noctua-dev and be stored''' before it can be saved: both files are exported from the stored model. The agent stores it for you; nothing is submitted from notes or a hand-written file.
* Only complete models are accepted, and only listed GO members can submit.

=== Ground rules ===

* '''The agent works on noctua-dev, never on production Noctua.''' Reviewing a production model is read-only. Edits to production are made by the model's author, in Noctua, outside the hub.
* '''Do not edit models you did not create.''' noctua-dev is shared, and the server lets any token edit any model. If you want to try something on someone else's model, ask the agent to make a new model and work on that. If a model of yours looks touched by someone else, ask the agent to compare it with your drop-box copy and tell the operators.
* '''Check the work.''' The agent says which tool it is using. It can be wrong, and it must not claim to have read a paper from its abstract. Verify before you trust an annotation.
* '''Each session has its own temporary space.''' Every account is a real user on one shared machine; since 2026-10-15 each session's <code>/tmp</code> is private and emptied when the session ends. Keep anything you want under your home directory.
* '''This is a shared, monitored environment.''' Session logs and usage traces may be reviewed to improve the service; session traces are also stored in an external analysis service (Langfuse). Do not store personal secrets or anything you would not want examined.

== What the GO AI hub does ==

Each user gets a Unix account on one machine, with JupyterLab (a file browser, editors and terminals) and the agent. The agent has skills (below) for GO curation and ontology editing, a term-search connection to the Ontology Lookup Service, and your GitHub identity. It builds and reviews GO-CAM models, searches the literature and the protein databases, edits the ontology in a personal checkout of [https://github.com/geneontology/go-ontology go-ontology], and opens issues and pull requests, all without asking you first. Read what it reports.

== Skills ==

A skill is a set of instructions and small tools that teach the agent one job. The hub serves them from two repositories and links them into every account; the agent picks the right one from what you ask, or you can name one.

{| class="wikitable"
! Skill !! Use it for
|-
| <code>amigo</code> || existing annotations and gene products, in the identifiers Noctua uses
|-
| <code>annotate-function</code> || a literature-driven review of a gene's GO function annotations, with proposed changes
|-
| <code>gene-review</code> || a full review of one gene's GO annotations (accept, modify, remove, add), written to a validated review file; ''review DANRE fezf2''
|-
| <code>gocam-best-practice</code> || the annotation guidelines for creating, editing or checking a GO-CAM model
|-
| <code>noctua</code> || reading and editing models in the GO-CAM store (noctua-dev), including the token step
|-
| <code>pubmed-eutils</code> || PubMed searches and article metadata, by PMID
|-
| <code>save-to-drop-box</code> || saving a finished model to go-cam-drop-box as a pull request
|-
| <code>uniprot-database</code> || UniProt searches, sequences and identifier mapping
|-
| <code>chemical-entity</code>, <code>reaction</code>, <code>design-pattern</code>, <code>external-term-lookup</code>, <code>mapping</code>, <code>taxon-constraint</code>, <code>term-obsoletion</code>, <code>pr-review</code>, <code>research</code> || ontology editing, from [https://github.com/geneontology/go-ontology go-ontology]: CHEBI and catalytic-activity terms, design patterns for new terms, term lookup in external ontologies, mappings, taxon constraints, obsoletion, reviewing an ontology pull request, and background research into a RESEARCH.md
|}

=== Invoking skills ===

Just ask: the agent chooses the skill. To name one, type its name with a leading slash, for example <code>/save-to-drop-box</code>.

=== Changes to skills ===

Skills live in [https://github.com/geneontology/go-skills go-skills] (curation) and go-ontology (ontology editing). A change merged there reaches every account within minutes. The copies in your account are read-only links; a copy you edit in your home reaches nobody else.

=== Modifying skills ===

To propose a change, ask the agent to open an issue against go-skills, or a pull request from a fork; the repository's issue forms guide the request. A skill of your own, written in your account under another name, works for you and is left alone; ask the agent to propose it to go-skills when you want everyone to have it.

== Getting help and giving feedback ==

* '''Something went wrong, or the agent did something odd:''' ask the agent to write up what happened, then report it. Problems with a skill or its documentation go to [https://github.com/geneontology/go-skills/issues go-skills issues]; problems with the hub itself (sign-in, sessions, missing tools) to [https://github.com/geneontology/go-jupyter/issues go-jupyter issues]. The agent can file either under your name. Pascale Gaudet also keeps an error-report sheet and a skill-suggestion sheet for curators; ask her for them. <!-- CHECK: replace with the two links once Pascale says they may be listed. -->
* A <code>/report</code> command inside the agent, to flag a moment for later review without leaving the session, is planned ([https://github.com/geneontology/go-jupyter/issues/25 go-jupyter#25]).
* '''Access and accounts:''' Kimberly Van Auken or Pascale Gaudet.

== Glossary ==

; GitHub : where GO's code, ontology and model submissions live, and the identity you sign in with. The agent acts there under your name.
; Pull request : a proposed change to a GitHub repository, reviewed before it is merged. Saving a model to the drop box opens one.
; noctua-dev : the test instance of Noctua, the GO-CAM editor and model store. Where the agent builds and edits models. Not a save.
; go-cam-drop-box : the public repository that receives finished models as pull requests; merged models move into production at a Noctua maintenance window.
; Token (barista token) : the personal key, obtained by signing in to noctua-dev with your ORCID, that lets the agent write models under your name. It expires; the agent asks again when it does.
; Skill : a set of instructions and tools that teach the agent one job (above).
; Session : your running environment on the hub, from the moment your server starts until it stops or the hub restarts. Files in your home directory outlast it; its temporary space does not.

